course: dictionary
lesson: 2771
title: Discus: a disc and a sport
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Discus is a noun with two meanings. It can mean the heavy flat disc that athletes throw, or the sport and event of throwing it. Context tells you which one is meant.
explain: «The Olympic Games began in 776 BC at Olympia... with running, wrestling, the discus and chariot races.» Here the discus sits in a list of sports, next to running and wrestling. So it means the sport, not the object.
explain: A good check is to swap in a meaning. In «throw the discus», the discus is the disc you throw, so the object meaning fits. In «the discus event», it is the sport, so the sport meaning fits.
fact: A discus is a heavy, flat, round disc that athletes throw as far as they can.
fact: Discus is also the name of the sport or event of throwing this disc.
fact: In ancient Greece, athletes threw the discus, and the famous statue «The Discus Thrower» shows one.
fact: The plural is usually discuses; disci is rare.
exercise: «The Olympic Games began in 776 BC... with running, wrestling, the discus and chariot races.» What does discus mean here? | A kind of chariot | A heavy flat disc that you throw | A Greek drink | *The sport of throwing a heavy disc
exercise: Lily says, «We are doing discus on sports day.» What does discus mean? | A round cake | A flat stone you skip across water | A jumping pit | *The event of throwing a heavy disc as far as you can
exercise: Which word means an athlete who throws the discus? | Referee | *Discus thrower | Discus event | Coach
test: In «The Discus Thrower», which word tells you the statue shows a person who throws? | The | Statue | *Thrower | Discus
test: What is a discus? | A type of wrestling hold | A small stone you skip across water | A kind of Greek drink | *A heavy flat round disc that athletes throw
exam: Mia says discus must mean a sort of hat, but the sentence is about throwing; what is the best way to check her idea? | Count the letters in the word | Ask nobody and just guess | *Swap in her meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense | Skip the word and never think about it again
